implementationPlan.description:
Docs for the new transport. First read the LANDED code (scripts/local-runner/guest-ssh.mjs, provision.mjs and lifecycle.mjs purge) and describe only what it does, never planned behavior.

1. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section 'Local Runner Isolation':
   - In the 'Supervisor modes' bullet, replace 'run as root through `multipass exec`' with 'run as root over the guest SSH route'.
   - Add one bullet 'Host-guest transport' covering:
     - all eight supervisor modes stream over the in-box OpenSSH client at a pinned path, never PATH: /usr/bin/ssh on Linux and macOS, %SystemRoot%\System32\OpenSSH\ssh.exe on Windows;
     - `-F none`, ed25519 only, the guest's host key pinned through cloud-init `ssh_keys` and checked under HostKeyAlias=<owned name>, no agent, forwarding, PTY or ProxyCommand;
     - a fresh per-VM client key at `<home>/.moe/guest-ssh/<name>.key` (0600, removed when the VM is purged). The guest accepts it only for the default user, as `restrict,command="exec /usr/bin/sudo -n /opt/moe/bin/moe-supervisor \"$SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND\""`, so it starts only supervisor modes;
     - `multipass exec` now carries only stdin-less queries capped at 64 KiB (MAX_PROBE_BUFFER);
     - the reason: Multipass 1.17.0 exec truncates stdin on Linux, delivers none on Windows (#3749) and stalls Windows stdout near 2 MiB (#5478);
     - error handling: there is no fallback to exec stdio or to the host. An unusable route fails closed with a fixed code.
   - Note that the job identities are denied the guest host key (readiness readHostKey).
2. docs/CERTIFICATION.md:
   - In the `bridge-framing` row, '2 MiB of frame bytes cross the guest SSH route exactly, in both directions.'
   - In 'How to run it' step 1, add that the host needs the in-box OpenSSH client at that pinned path (provisioning refuses with PROVISION_TRANSPORT_UNAVAILABLE otherwise), and that certify writes per-VM keys under `<home>/.moe/guest-ssh` and removes them with each VM.

Keep the Certified platforms table and every other row unchanged. Plain, short sentences.

